<!--
  The "mollet" egg (medium-boiled egg).

  French distinguishes three in-shell cookings, separated only by cooking time:
  "a la coque" (3 min, white barely set, liquid yolk), "mollet" (6 min, firm
  white, runny yolk, peeled egg), hard (9 min, everything set).

  Flavor Text has Soft-Boiled and Hard-Boiled, but nothing in between. The mollet
  is not a fuzzy middle ground: it is a preparation in its own right, the only
  one that is peeled and served on a plate rather than in an egg cup - on a
  salad, in a broth, in a red wine sauce (en meurette).

  It shares its signature (FT_Egg alone) with the other two, which is
  harmless: the engine draws at random among the matching defs, weighted
  by their specificity. Three defs for the same egg produce variety,
  not a conflict.
-->
<Defs>

	<FlavorText.FlavorDef ParentName="FlavorDef_Base">
		<defName>FlavorTextFR_OeufMollet</defName>
		<label>medium-boiled {0_coll}</label>
		<ingredients>
			<li><categories><li>FT_Egg</li></categories></li>
		</ingredients>
		<description>exactly six minutes in simmering water, then straight into ice. The white is firm, the yolk still runs. It is the only one of the three shell cookings that peels cleanly, and it wants patience and eggs that are not yesterday's.</description>
		<mealKinds>
			<li>FT_MealsNonSpecial</li>
			<li>FT_MealsSalad</li>
		</mealKinds>
	</FlavorText.FlavorDef>

</Defs>
